// A field borrowed through the reference a call returned is a place in what
// that reference points at -- here the caller's 'x' -- so it lives as long as
// the reference's type says, and the borrow is a '&i64' like '&x.n'.
//
// Verdict: accept; prints 5.
// Basis: language rule (1 Oct 2026) -- a borrow through another reference
// has the lifetime of what that reference points at, which is on the
// reference's type. Rust: 'let p = &id(&x).n;' accepted (by reading, not run).
// Master 8963c337: refused, ErrorInvType at 'printInt(*p)' -- the borrow was
// left untyped, as though the call's result were no place.

mod lifetime_band_field_through_call_result;

import stdio use *;

struct P { pub n i64; pub m i64; }

fn id(p &P) &P { p; }

fn main() {
  imm x = new P(5, 6);
  imm p = &id(&x).n;
  printInt(*p);
  printStr("\n");
}
